 "hypotheses": [
  {
   "#": "H1",
   "Root-cause hypothesis": "The radio / head-unit is the common failure point (a hub), not the individual symptoms",
   "Evidence from the data": "88% of claims end with the radio replaced - 80-94% in every symptom cluster (TF-IDF and embedding views), incl. camera, phone, satellite and display complaints; 31% of claims explicitly document an internal radio fault; only 2% show an external wiring/electrical cause",
   "Confidence": "High that the radio is the remedy; medium on the failure mechanism (narratives rarely state it)"
  },
  {
   "#": "H2",
   "Root-cause hypothesis": "Software / firmware robustness (programming and update) contributes materially",
   "Evidence from the data": "10% are classed software/programming; 7% were resolved by reprogramming WITHOUT replacing the radio; a USB/SPS programming topic covers 15% of narratives; complaints that mention a software update are 3% of claims in Apr-Dec 2020 and 8% in 2021 (exploratory: p=0.007 unadjusted, 0.073 after correction - not significant)",
   "Confidence": "Medium for a software contribution; the 2021 update-wording rise does not survive correction"
  },
  {
   "#": "H3",
   "Root-cause hypothesis": "The video / rear-camera signal path on specific model lines is weaker than on others",
   "Evidence from the data": "Camera/video complaints are over-represented in: FusionNova (18 claims vs 7 expected), HyperFury X (30 claims vs 17 expected), StellarGlide (16 claims vs 7 expected). In the independent repair text, FusionNova is over-represented in the repair topic 'Display / camera / coax diagnostics' (28 vs 14 expected). Yet 93% of camera claims are still fixed by replacing the radio",
   "Confidence": "Medium - convergent (symptom + repair text), but counts not rates"
  },
  {
   "#": "H4",
   "Root-cause hypothesis": "Diagnosis is not converging: radios are replaced on TAC advice without a documented cause",
   "Evidence from the data": "46% of claims replace the radio with no documented physical cause; 33% escalate to TAC/Techline and 92% of those end in replacement; 16% replace the radio while recording no fault codes",
   "Confidence": "High that documentation is thin; unknown how many replaced radios were truly faulty"
  },
  {
   "#": "H5",
   "Root-cause hypothesis": "Early-life (infant-mortality) failures escape end-of-line / dealer PDI testing",
   "Evidence from the data": "21% of claims occur within 90 days of the vehicle entering service; 76 claims are even dated BEFORE the in-service date (pre-delivery repairs?)",
   "Confidence": "Medium"
  },
  {
   "#": "H7",
   "Root-cause hypothesis": "An emerging power-management / sleep-mode problem: the radio fails to shut down after ignition-off and drains the battery",
   "Evidence from the data": "The ONLY change in event frequency that survives multiple-testing correction: 'stays on / battery drain' claims rise from 1% (2020Q3) to 11-12% (2021). They are 57% 'after ignition off', arrive later in life (median 309 vs 226 days), are fixed by reprogramming alone more often (11% vs 7%) and are escalated less (13% vs 34%)",
   "Confidence": "Medium-high that the increase is real; the mechanism (software sleep-mode vs hardware) is a hypothesis to test"
  },
  {
   "#": "H6",
   "Root-cause hypothesis": "Structured claim coding hides the real failure picture",
   "Evidence from the data": "The coded cause barely changes the repair or the evidence found (fig 5); coded complaint 'Communication' is mostly audio drop-outs and screen blanking, not connectivity; a hidden non-breaking-space defect split one complaint label in two",
   "Confidence": "High"
  }
 ],
